re than half  of the world's infertility problems male  infertility is really what we would call  a neglected global reproductive health  issue and it can actually be very  serious intractable uncurable chronic  it's often genetic in origin and so  after having written three books on  Egypt focusing mostly on women the last  one focusing a little bit on the men as  well I decided that I needed to do a  study in the Middle East of this large  problem of male infertility and that  ended up taking me to Lebanon for  complicated reasons but I went in the  year 2003 and did a very long and very  intensive study of Middle Eastern men  from Lebanon Palestine Syria and from a  number of other places to talk to them  about not only their infertility but  their support and commitment to their  infertile wives and in fact yes so that  was it is about infertility and men's  engagement with assisted reproductive  technologies to overcome it but I would  say the largest theme of this book well  beyond the infertility and the assisted  reproductive technologies in play in the  Middle East is really about  middle-eastern men's changing lives and  I think the the most important thing is  that really we have now hundreds of  books by scholars and and other people  describing various aspects of women's  lives in the Middle East most of them  women you know women's political  involvement women's changing you know  norms and practices and education and so  on but men  men Middle Eastern men as men really  have not been studied in terms of  masculinity their sense of self their  hopes for the future and so through this  lens of men who were going through  difficult issue with their wives I  learned so much about masculinity and  just about shifting generational norms  in the region and so I ended up writing  a book that's as much about Arab  masculinity as it is about treatment for  infertility and I think that is really  the big message of the book is that  there are emergent masculinities new  kinds of manhood shifting notions about  being a man in the region today and  that's what I think is the big message  of this book okay one of the things that  really surprised me that I think many  people are not aware of is the high rate  of infertility  what is the number basically globally if  you look at any given population in the  world this sort of average is anywhere  between ten and fifteen percent some of  the regions of the world have much  higher in fertility rates particularly  sub-saharan Africa for all sorts of  reasons the Middle Eastern region is  sort of in the global average I mean a  country like Egypt with about 80 million  people has about a 15 percent rate of  infertility among married couples  now within infertility generally  speaking it's said that about 30 to 40  percent of cases are female infertility  problems 30 to 40 percent of cases are  male infertility problems and then the  rest twenty percent or so our combined  male and female infertility problems so  if you take the male issues altogether  it's said that about half of all cases  of childlessness involve a male  infertility problem in the Middle East  if you go to IVF clinics across the  region the rates of male infertility are  much higher than that in clinics that  I've worked in it's between 60 70 % of  all cases I have heard from others that  there's some regions in the Middle East  where up to 90 percent of couples coming  into clinics there will be a male  infertility problem so male infertility  is a serious problem in the  middle-eastern region  and why is that do you think is there  any reason  yeah actually so now what's understood  about male infertility is that a lot of  it is genetic it's actually little  mutations often on the y chromosome they  can be spontaneous or they may be  related to just genetic defects and they  can run in families and so it's now  realized with you know increase things  are genetic sophistication that a lot of  male infertility has a genetic origin in  the Middle Eastern region and in other  regions of the world many regions of the  world there are high rates of what's  called consanguineous marriage where  people marry within their families okay  that cousins may marry each other first  cousins second cousins and that probably  increases the rates of male factor  infertility because genetic defects you  know that occur in close unions then get  passed on to offspring so what you will  find in a country like Lebanon is whole  families with you know several brothers  who are infertile several first cousins  uncles and so forth that there's  actually clustering of male infertility  and families and it's very devastating  for those families where you know  several men are having trouble  conceiving I met many of those cases in  the course of my research okay let's  talk about how you did your research  what was your methodology yes so for  about since in vitro fertilization IVF  which is the sort of base form of  assisted reproduction has come to the  region in the late 1980s it came to  Egypt Saudi Arabia and Jordan in 1986  I've been very fortunate to be able to  work in IVF clinics in a variety of  countries and often because very nice  IVF physicians have decided that they  were willing to let me come and do a  project in their clinical population so  I've worked in a number of IVF clinics  in in the Middle East as well as in the  Arab American population in Michigan and  I have you know sort of participated in  the lives of these clinics and I have  done very in-depth what we would call  anthropological or ethnographic  interviews with  hundreds of people I have now you know  at last count I have interviewed more  than 300 men both fertile and infertile  men from about 14 different  middle-eastern countries the majority  have been Egyptian Lebanese Syrian and  Palestinian I've done a lot of solo  interviews with just men I've done a lot  of solo interviews with women and I've  done hundreds of couple interviews  together so you know and often you know  people these are long interviews where  people open up and tell me many intimate  aspects of their lives  I'm going to say that must have been I  would have imagined and assumed that if  that would be very difficult for a man  to sit down and talk with a Western  woman so to speak about that kind of  thing how did you get past that I would  imagine hesitation or yeah so that  that's an interesting story actually  because I had always worked with women  throughout the 1980s and early 1990s  until I went back to do my first study  in in vitro fertilization clinics in  Cairo Egypt and they're intending only  to work with women I was sort of ushered  into these you know hospital rooms where  women were recovering from various IVF  procedures and their husbands were there  and so the husband's often didn't want  to leave and I ended up in about 40% of  my interviews in Egypt including  husbands in the interviews and that's  where first I discovered that there were  high rates of male infertility many of  these women were going through  procedures because their their husbands  were infertile and men were talkative  they wanted to talk you know and so  these were interviews with couples  together but I realized that there you  know that lack of hesitation on the part  of Egyptian men to talk to me about  their infertility problems was probably  a good sign that maybe I could do this  with men alone and I think we've often  held a lot of untested assumptions and  somehow women can only talk to women  about sensitive or intimate things that  men could only talk to men and I was  abiding by that thinking until I not  only you know the studies I did in Egypt  but I talked to some Middle Eastern male  colleagues and I said I really want to  do this study of male infertility in the  region I see it's a really big issue and  I think I should get a male graduate  student to go do the work and I actually  tried to get a male graduate student to  go do the work and a couple of my very  supportive middle-eastern born  colleagues said no no no you Marcia you  should go do this project because at my  stage of life you know being middle-aged  sort of maternal figure men are going to  in the Middle East will have a much more  difficult time telling other men that  they have reproductive or sexual  problems you as a Western woman quasi  doctor like maternal you're probably in  a much better position to get people to  talk to you and so I decided that that  might make sense and I wrote that into  my grant proposals you know and I guess  everyone believed me and I was able to  go do the project I will say however in  all of my years of working on this topic  you know it's more than 20 years now I  have never had a single woman not agreed  to talk to me I mean women often really  want to unburden themselves I've never  had anybody disagree or you know reject  being in one of my studies but with men  for the first time in my life I had  refusals you know outright refusal no I  don't have time no I just don't want to  talk about this I'm not in a good mood  and I can say in general it was it was  difficult for a lot of men I I felt like  a lot of men were in a very bad mood  about their situation situations you  know reproductively stressful lives for  a lot of men in the Middle East that  have been made more stressful in the  last couple years and so a lot of it was  men unburdening the things they had been  through I mean you know a lot of  Lebanese in Palestinian and now Syrian  men have been through war often as you  know civilians they have had to flee  their lives are very hard economically  difficult and so a lot of the book is  really about you know just the stresses  of life living in a region where  everything is pretty unstable in your  prologue you talk about  man named Hamza and I think he really  does pay it's a very interesting picture  so why don't you tell us about him yeah  he was I called him Hamza he was a  driver that I ended up you know not only  having him drive me and my little family  I had my husband and two young children  with me in Lebanon but he ended up being  really he became a friend over time and  his story was pretty typical of the  story of a lot of Lebanese young men he  he was a Shia Muslim he grew up in a  sort of poor part of southern Lebanon  his family his father was involved in  one of the Shia political parties and  then when the war broke out they were  like 10 children in the family the war  broke out his father was taken from the  car and killed leaving a widowed woman  with all of these children to support  and so worried that the boys of the  family were going to be conscripted into  militias Hamza like many young Lebanese  men fled he he went actually to the Arab  Gulf and he began working there helped  to support his family back in Lebanon  and he like many young Middle Eastern  men he chose to marry his cousin which  is just a very preferred form of  marriage in many parts of the world and  so she moved to the Gulf with him where  they discovered that you know there was  no pregnancy forthcoming and basically  he it was discovered over time that he  had a pretty serious male factor  infertility problem but like many  childless couples Arab couples they  really loved each other you know it was  a very committed relationship very  loving precious relationship you know as  he put it we have an understanding with  each other she didn't want to leave him  she loved him he didn't want to leave  her even though often when people are  the infertile one they'll often tell  their partner look at your free to  divorce I don't want to saddle you with  my problem and most often people say no  no you're more precious to me than  having children I'm going to stick with  you and so they worked for a long time  to save up money  and eventually to build a home for  themselves when things settle down in  Lebanon and I just happen to meet him  you know he was a taxi driver and you  certain discover in the Middle East when  you ask a series of polite questions  that when people have been married for a  while and there are no children  you know automatically that there's some  sort of an infertility problem because  within marriage it's almost mandatory to  start having children and so he'd been  married for a long time there were no  kids and so I told him what I was doing  there and I ended up you know figuring  out very quickly what the problem was  and I ended up helping him I explained  to him you really really need to use an  assistive reproductive technology to  overcome this problem you'll not get  pregnant otherwise and so I got involved  in his journey to assisted Teknik  reproductive technologies which are  expensive they're physically taxing it  wasn't easy and during the process of  all of this sort of driving back and  forth and helping him get to clinics and  meeting his wife and his family at one  point I realized that among the Shia  most slums of Lebanon that people could  go one of two ways they and one of the  ways that you could go was to be a  sympathizer or a member of Hezbollah the  Shia Muslim political organization which  officially is regarded by the US  government as a as a terrorist  organization and so it's one long drive  back from southern Lebanon I finally  came to that moment of saying gosh I'm  saying you know did you follow this  person or that person and then I figured  out well he was sympathetic with  Hizbollah and we had a very interesting  conversation he said no I'm not a  terrorist I don't want you to think I'm  a bad person  I said you know no no I understand you  know a lot about what Hezbollah does for  poor people in the south of Lebanon and  you know as they do offer a lot of  social they offer offer a lot of social  services especially in the kind of  absence of an official government and so  you know it was this moment of sort of  like well this is just she's a good man  he's my friend he loves my children and  I his political convictions are what  they  our but we have come to an understanding  with each other and I realized you know  in a way this was part of sort of  humanizing middle-eastern man Arab men  who in the Western media you know and in  in scholarship as well they're always  sort of cast in one way especially in  Western media we only hear bad stories  about terrorist religious zealots you  know armed protesters violent men who  make women veil or keep them in purdah  you know we hear many things about  Middle Eastern men Arab men and many of  those things are just caricatures about  who men are and so the book if anything  is just I I think it's a humanizing  account of you know somebody like humza  who yes he sympathizes with Hezbollah  for various reasons but he's a real  flesh and blood kind person who arrived  at that point in his life for you know  certain reasons and the book I think has  many many accounts of men's lives that  you end up thinking well they're just  she no normal human beings like like  everyone else right right and the hamza  story does have a happy ending yes it  does actually after repeated trials of  this reproductive technology there's a  one reproductive technology called XE  intracytoplasmic sperm injection it's a  variant of IVF especially designed to  overcome male infertility he and his  wife tried it three times they had to  move clinics and finally the third trial  was the charm and you know I got a very  early morning call I was in back in the  States from a very excited humza saying  you know doctora guess what happened you  know she's pregnant and indeed on one of  my trips back to Lebanon in about 2009  I you know went to meet him and there  was a beautiful little girl there so  they had had you know a successful  accede pregnancy and a birth of a child  so it was a great ending to that story I  have to say the sort of sad part about  these technologies I mean  I'm sort of although I I see the joy  when they work and there is a lot of joy  when they do work these are not  ultimately highly successful  technologies they're very expensive and  globally assisted reproduction in the  best centers under the best conditions  only works up to 40% of the times and  that's actually a very you know positive  sort of figure for people who have more  serious infertility problems the older  you get the percentage of success  success Goes Down and so they're  ultimately they're the only solution for  a lot of cases of infertility like his  and he was very lucky to have that child  because he had a pretty serious male  infertility problem but this sort of  what shall we say the sort of downside  or the dark side of assisted  reproduction is that it often doesn't  work and in fact a lot of the men in my  study had gone through repeated trials  of XE and IVF and there had nothing to  show for it well your book does show a  very different portrayal of the era of  man versus the stereotypes that we  typically tend to gravitate towards  though thank you very much for being  here today and sharing some of your work  no thank you it's been a pleasure for  more information about Professor in  Hoorn and her research please visit our  website at yale.edu slash macmillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

