ike well this is just she's a good man  he's my friend he loves my children and  I his political convictions are what  they  our but we have come to an understanding  with each other and I realized you know  in a way this was part of sort of  humanizing middle-eastern man Arab men  who in the Western media you know and in  in scholarship as well they're always  sort of cast in one way especially in  Western media we only hear bad stories  about terrorist religious zealots you  know armed protesters violent men who  make women veil or keep them in purdah  you know we hear many things about  Middle Eastern men Arab men and many of  those things are just caricatures about  who men are and so the book if anything  is just I I think it's a humanizing  account of you know somebody like humza  who yes he sympathizes with Hezbollah  for various reasons but he's a real  flesh and blood kind person who arrived  at that point in his life for you know  certain reasons and the book I think has  many many accounts of men's lives that  you end up thinking well they're just  she no normal human beings like like  everyone else right right and the hamza  story does have a happy ending yes it  does actually after repeated trials of  this reproductive technology there's a  one reproductive technology called XE  intracytoplasmic sperm injection it's a  variant of IVF especially designed to  overcome male infertility he and his  wife tried it three times they had to  move clinics and finally the third trial  was the charm and you know I got a very  early morning call I was in back in the  States from a very excited humza saying  you know doctora guess what happened you  know she's pregnant and indeed on one of  my trips back to Lebanon in about 2009  I you know went to meet him and there  was a beautiful little girl there so  they had had you know a successful  accede pregnancy and a birth of a child  so it was a great ending to that story I  have to say the sort of sad part about  these technologies I mean  I'm sort of although I I see the joy  when they work and there is a lot of joy  when they do work these are not  ultimately highly successful  technologies they're very expensive and  globally assisted reproduction in the  best centers under the best conditions  only works up to 40% of the times and  that's actually a very you know positive  sort of figure for people who have more  serious infertility problems the older  you get the percentage of success  success Goes Down and so they're  ultimately they're the only solution for  a lot of cases of infertility like his  and he was very lucky to have that child  because he had a pretty serious male  infertility problem but this sort of  what shall we say the sort of downside  or the dark side of assisted  reproduction is that it often doesn't  work and in fact a lot of the men in my  study had gone through repeated trials  of XE and IVF and there had nothing to  show for it well your book does show a  very different portrayal of the era of  man versus the stereotypes that we  typically tend to gravitate towards  though thank you very much for being  here today and sharing some of your work  no thank you it's been a pleasure for  more information about Professor in  Hoorn and her research please visit our  website at yale.edu slash macmillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale
 

